================================================================================
                     EX01 — Form up, maggots!
================================================================================

WHAT THIS EXERCISE COVERS:
  - Everything from ex00 (Bureaucrat stays the same, + one new method)
  - A NEW class: Form
  - Class interaction: Bureaucrat signs a Form
  - Two separate exception systems (Bureaucrat has its own, Form has its own)
  - const attributes in a class
  - beSigned() — a method on Form that takes a Bureaucrat
  - signForm() — a method on Bureaucrat that takes a Form
  - CIRCULAR DEPENDENCY between headers (forward declaration)

GRADE SYSTEM REMINDER:
  - Grade 1   = HIGHEST (best)
  - Grade 150 = LOWEST  (worst)
  - A bureaucrat can sign a form ONLY if his grade <= gradeToSign
    (lower number = higher authority)

================================================================================
                    YOUR COMPILE ERROR — EXPLAINED
================================================================================

  ERROR:
    Form.hpp:30: error: 'Bureaucrat' does not name a type

  WHY IT HAPPENS:
    You have a CIRCULAR INCLUDE:
      Bureaucrat.hpp → #include "Form.hpp"
      Form.hpp       → #include "Bureaucrat.hpp"

    When the compiler processes main.cpp:
      1. main.cpp includes Bureaucrat.hpp
      2. Bureaucrat.hpp includes Form.hpp
      3. Form.hpp tries to include Bureaucrat.hpp
      4. But #ifndef BUREAUCRAT_HPP blocks it (already being processed)
      5. Form.hpp sees "Bureaucrat" in beSigned() → NOT DECLARED YET → ERROR

  HOW TO FIX:
    In Bureaucrat.hpp:
      - REMOVE: #include "Form.hpp"
      - ADD:    class Form;          (this is a "forward declaration")

    A forward declaration says "trust me, Form exists, I'll define it later."
    This works because signaForm(Form& f) only uses a REFERENCE.
    For references/pointers, the compiler doesn't need the full class — just
    the name is enough.

    Form.hpp KEEPS #include "Bureaucrat.hpp" because beSigned() calls
    b.getGrade() — it needs the FULL class definition.

  CONCEPT TO UNDERSTAND:
    Forward declaration = "I promise this class exists"
    #include = "Here is the full class definition"
    Use forward declaration when you only need a pointer or reference.
    Use #include when you need to call methods or know the size.

================================================================================
                       REMAINING BUGS IN YOUR CODE
================================================================================

[BUG 1] COMPILE ERROR — Circular include (see above)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  In Bureaucrat.hpp, replace:
      #include "Form.hpp"
  With:
      class Form;

  That's it. One line change fixes the compile error.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 2] what() methods don't RETURN anything
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your Form.cpp has:
      const char *Form::GradeTooLowException::what() const{
          std::cout << "Form GradeTosign or to Execute too low\n";
      }

  PROBLEMS:
    1. what() must RETURN a const char*, not cout it.
       The caller decides what to do with the message.
    2. Missing throw() specifier (required in C++98)

  Should be:
      const char *Form::GradeTooLowException::what() const throw(){
          return "Form grade to sign or execute is too low";
      }

  Same fix for GradeTooHighException.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 3] operator<< for Form — doesn't show useful info
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code:
      os << b.getName() << " Has Signed a form\n";

  This doesn't match the subject. operator<< should print ALL the form info:
      Form <name>, signed: yes/no, grade to sign: <X>, grade to execute: <Y>

  The operator<< is for DISPLAYING the form's state, not for saying
  someone signed it. That's signForm()'s job.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 4] Bureaucrat increment/decrement still have old bugs
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your ex01/Bureaucrat.cpp still has the WRONG increment/decrement logic
  from the original code (double checks). You fixed it in ex00 but didn't
  carry the fix over to ex01.

  incrementGrade() should only check: if (grade <= 1) throw TooHigh; grade--;
  decrementGrade() should only check: if (grade >= 150) throw TooLow; grade++;

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 5] Bureaucrat operator<< — same typo "gureaucrat"
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Copy your fixed version from ex00 (but still has "gureaucrat" there too).
  Should be: ", bureaucrat grade "

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 6] Bureaucrat copy constructor — same self-check issue
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Same as ex00: the self-check (&other != this) is pointless in a copy
  constructor. And you should initialize grade in the initializer list too.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 7] Default Form constructor initializes gradeToSign/Execute to 0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code:
      Form::Form() : gradeToExecute(0), gradeToSign(0)

  But 0 is an INVALID grade (valid range is 1-150). If you need a default
  constructor, use grade 150 (lowest valid) or throw. Having 0 means you
  create an object that violates your own rules.

