================================================================================
                            TRAPS / PITFALLS
================================================================================

  TRAP 1: Circular includes (THIS IS WHAT BROKE YOUR BUILD)
    Form.hpp needs Bureaucrat → keep #include "Bureaucrat.hpp"
    Bureaucrat.hpp needs Form → use forward declaration "class Form;"
    RULE: use forward declaration when you only need a reference/pointer.

  TRAP 2: what() must RETURN, not print
    The what() method returns a string. The CALLER prints it.
    If you cout inside what(), you break the catch block's logic.
    e.what() is called inside the catch → the catch decides how to display.

  TRAP 3: throw() specifier in C++98
    In C++98, exception methods need: const char* what() const throw();
    This tells the compiler "this function promises not to throw."
    Without it, some compilers give warnings/errors with -Werror.

  TRAP 4: signForm message format
    Subject says:
      Success: "<bureaucrat> signed <form>"
      Failure: "<bureaucrat> couldn't sign <form> because <reason>"
    Your version says "failed to sign" — that's fine but check the subject
    wording for your school's correction.

  TRAP 5: beSigned checks <=, not <
    Grade 50 bureaucrat CAN sign a form with gradeToSign 50.
    You got this right — just don't accidentally change it.

  TRAP 6: operator= for Form
    You don't have one declared. Since all const members can't be assigned,
    you might want to either declare it private or just not implement it.
    The compiler-generated one won't work with const members.
